<Project>
  <!-- Your app. Every build takes its ids and its name from these two values:

         com.example.myvpn.android          the Google Play build
         com.example.myvpn.android.web      the APK your website hands out
         com.example.myvpn.ios              the App Store build, with its network extension
                                            (…ios.networkextension) and App Group (group.…ios)
         com.example.myvpn.windows.web      the Windows build
         com.example.myvpn.linux.web        the Linux build

       Choose the base once: the stores key your listings on these ids, and an installed app is known
       by them, so they must not change after your first release.

       Your release files, the Windows executable and service, and the Linux binary and its service
       are called by the name's letters and digits (MyVPN). To call them otherwise, add
       <VhAppPackageTitle>YourName</VhAppPackageTitle> below - also before your first release.

       Company is you, as the app's maker: the consent summary and the other documents the app shows
       name you by it, and so do the executables' details, with a copyright made from it.

       Version is your app's version, in three numbers, which the app shows and compares its updates
       with. Its third number counts your releases: 1 for the first, as it stands, and one more for each after it,
       committed with the release. Never set it back, even when you raise the first two (1.0.7, then
       1.1.8): it is Android's version code and iOS's build number, which a store takes only higher,
       and a phone installs no update from your website with a lower one. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VhAppIdBase>com.example.myvpn</VhAppIdBase>
    <VhAppName>My VPN</VhAppName>
    <Company>My Company</Company>
    <Version>1.0.1</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
